/* Reference: Basic Pipelining Stages (RISC Example) */
IF (Instruction Fetch)
ID (Instruction Decode/Reg Fetch)
EX (Execute/ALU Operation)
MEM (Memory Access)
WB (Write Back to Register)

/* Hazard Types (Pipelining Stalls) */
Structural Hazard: Hardware cannot support instruction combination (e.g., resource conflict).
Data Hazard (RAW, WAW, WAR): Dependency on data not yet written back.
Control Hazard: Branch prediction failure or jump target uncertainty.

/* Optimization: Loop Unrolling (C to Assembly Concept) */
// C Original
for (i=0; i < N; i++) { sum += A[i]; }

// Optimized (Unrolled by factor 4)
i=0; while (i < N-3) {
    sum += A[i];
    sum += A[i+1];
    sum += A[i+2];
    sum += A[i+3];
    i += 4;
}
// Handle remainder...

/* Unrolling Benefit: Reduces Branch Overhead */
One branch/comparison per 4 iterations
Increases Instruction Level Parallelism (ILP)
Requires more registers for temporary storage
